sections.body:
Nearly every controller in Kubernetes -- built-in or custom -- follows the same generic shape, often summarized as: observe, diff, act, repeat.

```python
while True:
    desired = api_server.get(kind="ReplicaSet", name="web")
    actual_pods = api_server.list(kind="Pod", label_selector=desired.selector)
    diff = desired.spec.replicas - len(actual_pods)
    if diff > 0:
        for _ in range(diff):
            api_server.create(kind="Pod", template=desired.spec.template)
    elif diff < 0:
        for pod in actual_pods[:abs(diff)]:
            api_server.delete(pod)
    wait_for_next_relevant_event()
```

The crucial design detail, easy to miss on a first read, is that this loop is **level-triggered**, not edge-triggered: on every single pass, it recomputes the diff from scratch by re-reading the *current* desired state and the *current* actual state -- it never asks 'what specific event just happened' or relies on remembering what it was previously in the middle of doing. In practice this loop isn't literally a `while True` busy-spin; controllers are woken by watch events (an object was created, updated, or deleted) and also run a periodic full resync as a safety net in case an event was somehow missed, but the *logic* executed on each wake-up is exactly this recompute-the-diff-from-current-state shape, regardless of what triggered it.

